l day,' there has to be drama or there's no point in this being a story. So, the Friendships we see are normally ones under a lot of stress. CuChulain and his fellow Ulster Heroes are all thick as thieves, and in the Tain we see these Friendships put to the test as situations conspire to have CuChulain's friends set against him. Fergus mac Roich is a friend / father figure to CuChulain, and even though the pair are on opposite sides due to Fergus being incapable of making good life choices, they are still good friends who respect each other. There are several points where they both help one and other even to their own detriment. Conall Cernach is an older hero, but a ferocious friend of CuChulain who goes on the warpath when he is killed, slaughtering everyone who had anything to do with it. And then there's Fer Diad who CuChulain is <i>very close friends</i> with, hugging, kissing, sleeping in the same bed close. That's a whole other thing, but yeah.<br /> <br /> So, Friendship is pretty much just like ours, except set in a narrative so constantly under threat due to the needs of a narrative for drama. <br /> <br /> Now, Social Media is something something I hadn't considered before. The Tuatha would be those really overly dramatic people always up to shit more or less, but the idea of following, unfollowing, and that sort of thing, <i>that</i> would be a mess. That stuff would be taken dangerously seriously. Unfriending someone would be a really fierce social declaration that could really sour relationships and cause knock-on effects through a tribe. Friending someone could equally cause social friction. What would probably happen is everyone had very specific outwardly facing friend lists, friends with folks you didn't like, not friending friends of yours who would cause political messes. Unfriending someone who was above you in social rank would be a <i>mess</i>, I don't have any real textual examples to look to, but breaking up of friendships, or airing political dity laundry in the public like that's not something that would work well within the tight knit, incredibly dramatic social system we see in the texts.<br /> <br /> <b>Modern Society</b><br /> Now, love the Tuatha as I do, their ideas of social relationships wouldn't work awesomely in modern society due to how their society was structured. Kings at the top, noble warriors below them, then trained professionals, everyone else, and then slaves. They were an <i>incredibly</i> classed society, complicated as shit. They were working with a class system that was, while theoretically mobile to a degree, so strict that your legal rights depended on what your rank was. Add to that the fact that the top of the social ladder is a Divine King, something modern society doesn't click much with, it would probably be rather tricky.<br /> <br /> The biggest problem, however, is the exaltation of violence. The society the Tuatha are from / are an echo of / are a fabricated reference to (depending on what theory floats your boat) a society that exalted violence. Killing, raiding, taking slaves, all of this was a primary feature of society, and shaped the structuring of their society. Killing someone for wronging you is totally not cool modernly, but totally a legitimate (if not always legal) idea for the Tuatha. Add to that their Honor-Price system, and you get an even trickier class problem. The wealthier you are, the more you can inflict physical or social damage on other people by simply paying a fine. Implement this in our modern society with such a massive disparity between the wealthy and the not, and you would have the wealthy totally able to kill, maim, and worse to people with no real recourse for it. They would pay the fine, but it would be pocket change.<br /> <br /> <b>Divine and Mortal Interactions</b><br /> Relationships between the Tuatha, their Scions, and Mortals would be a bit messy. For one, the Tuatha are a raiding, slaving, colonizing people who don't see anything wrong with that at all. Add to that the fact that they probably <i>really want</i> to take Ireland back from the Sons of Mil means that there would likely be conflict. The Tuatha are colonizers, Irish mythology is a story of mass colonization, and the Tuatha would be more than happy to just roll up on mortal Ireland and subjugate the mortal population. In my games, what I have generally keeping this at bay is all of the big names being dead. But, honestly, the danger to Ireland isn't the Fomori, it's the Tuatha. The Fomori want to tax people, and have client kingdoms. The Tuatha want <i>Ireland</i>, that's all they want, and they have slaughtered native populations (Fir Bolg) to do it before, and would be willing to do it again. The Tuatha are a dominating force, they want Ireland, and will accept no other options.<br /> <br /> Now, ignoring all the problems with slavery, explotation of lower classes, strict social higherarchy, class systems, and general yucky social systems like that, there is one other big challenge with the relationship. The Tuatha, and their Scions, live these really dramatic, over the top lives. They are The Best and they are The Worst. There is no capacity of being in the middle ground, everything is Awful and Awesome. You fiercely defend your own supremacy, all the while trying to usurp others to nick their honor from them. It is a rather vicious social system, the focus on the best and greatest would create conflict everywhere. Every Tuatha Scion who doesn't win Best Actor would be up there drawing knives and walking on stage. The one who does win Best Actor is probably horrifically pissed they didn't also win Best Supporting Actor, Best Director, and Best Editor. The Olympics would suddenly be a <i>very dangerous</i> place to be. Any contest would be taken lethally seriously. From county fair, to the Nobel Peace Prize, the Tuatha <i>need </i>to be seen to be awesome and better than everyone else, including other members of the Tuath.<br /> <br /> <b>In Sum</b><br /> Ignoring the Social System of the Tuatha, on an individual level they would fit into modern society fairly well. They would be behind the times with women, sexuality, and that sort of thing, but they wouldn't be too bad. The big challenges is that their Social System is something that most modern people would be very uncomfortable with, the fact that they are more than willing to purge Ireland, or enslave it's population, and the fact that they have no chill.<br /> <br /> The Theoi are often described as a bunch of teenagers given Superpowers, the Tuatha are like if you removed someone's capacity to do anything not to the maximum passionate level, gave them super powers, and made them regard violence as a totally okay solution to most problems.</div> </div> </div> </div>Watcher,Ive noticed the scion preview mentioned the possibility of the fey being older than the tuatha. But Ive also heard that the fey at\u00e9 the descendents of the gods. What do you think is true?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-15T17:43:51-07:00","id":1140682,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/155803-scion-2-0-project?p=1140679#post1140679\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Watcher,Ive noticed the scion preview mentioned the possibility of the fey being older than the tuatha. But Ive also heard that the fey at\u00e9 the descendents of the gods. What do you think is true?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it's fair to interpret it either way, the Sidhe (Irish word for Fey, Fairy, or Elf. Sort of. It actually is just a word for the Hollow Hills these beings live in, but they get commonly called the Sidhe after their housing) as something older than the Tuatha, or possibly their descendants, or even the Tuatha themselves. It's sort of a tricky situation with them. The Sidhe are originally just the Otherworldly People who reside in the Sidhe Mounds, the Hollow Hills that dotted the Mythic Landscape of Ireland. The Tuatha are sometimes described as Sidhe, while other times, Sidhe are described as being something that is somehow different from the Deities. Manannan sends Sidhe Knights with Lugh when he rides out with his foster brothers to fight Balor, suggesting they are somehow different from the Tuatha. At the same time, in The Intoxication of the Ulstermen, you see The Dagda and some of the other Tuatha described as being Sidhe, unseen people capable of strange things.<br /> <br /> Strictly, based on the Book of Invasions, the Sidhe could only be older than the Tuatha if they were something to do with the Fir Bolg. All of the other, elder peoples, are dead or dead enough that there's not enough people to remain a power. But, they are complicated enough a subject that it's totally up to the authors to decide how to best use them. They are particularly tricky, one of the most difficult Legendary Beings to acomodate in Scion.<br /> <br /> One of the biggest challenges is honestly that the Sidhe everyone likes to talk about are from the medieval period, and totally non-mythic. Including them is a lot of fun, but they technically are not really part of Irish Mythology. The actual 'truth' about the Sidhe is probably that it's more or less a word that means 'Non-Human Human-Like Supernatural Beings Who Reside In The Sidhe Mounds,' including the Deities, 'Fairy Queens' like Fand and Niamh, and minor entities like the Dullahan. It's less of a species, and more of a really broad term.<br /> <br /> However, that's literally an idea I just came up with right now inspired by how the Haida use a term roughly meaning 'Supernatural Beings' to describe everything from big Deities, to 'smaller' entities. Any interpretation of the Sidhe is okay more or less, they're a tricky thing to deal with. It's a term that has obviously changed meaning over the last thousand years. The way I tend to depict it in my games is that there is a species of Supernatural Beings who are the Sidhe, sort of like minor tribal groups with their own Monarchs, more or less subserviant to the Tuatha.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 09-15-2017, 08:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-15T18:18:10-07:00","id":1140688,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/155803-scion-2-0-project?p=1140682#post1140682\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think it's fair to interpret it either way, the Sidhe (Irish word for Fey, Fairy, or Elf. Sort of. It actually is just a word for the Hollow Hills these beings live in, but they get commonly called the Sidhe after their housing) as something older than the Tuatha, or possibly their descendants, or even the Tuatha themselves. It's sort of a tricky situation with them. The Sidhe are originally just the Otherworldly People who reside in the Sidhe Mounds, the Hollow Hills that dotted the Mythic Landscape of Ireland. The Tuatha are sometimes described as Sidhe, while other times, Sidhe are described as being something that is somehow different from the Deities. Manannan sends Sidhe Knights with Lugh when he rides out with his foster brothers to fight Balor, suggesting they are somehow different from the Tuatha. At the same time, in The Intoxication of the Ulstermen, you see The Dagda and some of the other Tuatha described as being Sidhe, unseen people capable of strange things.<br /> <br /> Strictly, based on the Book of Invasions, the Sidhe could only be older than the Tuatha if they were something to do with the Fir Bolg. All of the other, elder peoples, are dead or dead enough that there's not enough people to remain a power. But, they are complicated enough a subject that it's totally up to the authors to decide how to best use them. They are particularly tricky, one of the most difficult Legendary Beings to acomodate in Scion.<br /> <br /> One of the biggest challenges is honestly that the Sidhe everyone likes to talk about are from the medieval period, and totally non-mythic. Including them is a lot of fun, but they technically are not really part of Irish Mythology. The actual 'truth' about the Sidhe is probably that it's more or less a word that means 'Non-Human Human-Like Supernatural Beings Who Reside In The Sidhe Mounds,' including the Deities, 'Fairy Queens' like Fand and Niamh, and minor entities like the Dullahan. It's less of a species, and more of a really broad term.<br /> <br /> However, that's literally an idea I just came up with right now inspired by how the Haida use a term roughly meaning 'Supernatural Beings' to describe everything from big Deities, to 'smaller' entities. Any interpretation of the Sidhe is okay more or less, they're a tricky thing to deal with. It's a term that has obviously changed meaning over the last thousand years. The way I tend to depict it in my games is that there is a species of Supernatural Beings who are the Sidhe, sort of like minor tribal groups with their own Monarchs, more or less subserviant to the Tuatha.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you. Now just more thing,if you don't mind. The annuna. You mentioned on a thread of mine that the the annuna fought another group called the annunaki. Can you tell me their story? What was the fight about? And who was in each side of 5he conflict?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-15T19:41:39-07:00","id":1140701,"title":"","content":"Since Brighid is the Irish Goddess prayed to for computer problems it would logical that a scion related to her would become the deity of the internet. So my question is how would you work such a individual considering 2.0 does not have a Forge purview?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13647-manannansun","name":"Manannansun","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"78"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-15T20:38:45-07:00","id":1140711,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/155803-scion-2-0-project?p=1140688#post1140688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thank you. Now just more thing,if you don't mind. The annuna. You mentioned on a thread of mine that the the annuna fought another group called the annunaki. Can you tell me their story? What was the fight about? And who was in each side of 5he conflict?</div> </div> </div> </div>Happy to help! I am lucky enough to have an enormous amount of resources available to me, so it's only right that I use them to help answer people's questions about mythology and history.<br /> <br /> I double checked my use of the terms Anunnaki and Anunna before I responded, and it looks like I messed up. I had thought the -ki on Anunnaki was a suffix for 'Great', which it does not appear to, essentially breaking Anunnaki into 'Great Gods,' and Anunna simply into 'Gods'. What I was meaning to elude to there was the Akkadian text titled <i>Atra-Hasis </i>which discusses the ordering of the world. In this version, we have the Anunnaki described as the greater Gods (though this title changes and shifts over time in meaning, and members of this group) coming into conflict with 'Lesser Gods' essentially. <br /> <br /> More or less, this is how it happens. Reality is being ordered, everything's being sorted out. Anu, Enlil, and Enki divide up the world around them, one ruling the skies, another the earth, and another the waters. (This may sound very suspiciously familiar, and it should be! Mesopotamian religious beliefs were very influential in the Eastern Mediterranean world) Now, the Anunnaki don't want to do menial labor because they're important and that's a bunch of nonsense, so they round up the Minor Deities and go, &quot;Right, you lot, congratulations, you just became an Agrarian Underclass,&quot; and set them to farming and managing agricultural activities to feed the Anunnaki.<br /> <br /> This, predictably, leads to a revolt. It doesn't go well, the Anunnaki are Great Gods for a reason. However, everyone's a bit uncomfortable about this entire situation, the Lesser Gods feel like they are being abused and exploited, so everyone takes a moment to think. Eventually, they come up with the idea of making a new set of laborers for the Gods, allowing the Lesser Gods to join the Major Gods in doing... whatever it is that Cosmic Deities get up to. Who is this new slave race? Humanity. Helpfully, <i>very weak</i> so the Gods don't need to worry about anyone feeling uppity.<br /> <br /> Exactly who is a member of the Anunnaki is complicated, mostly because the Mesopotamians shift traditions city to city, and empire to empire. So, in the <i>Atra-Hasis</i>, we have the line, &quot;The seven great Anunnaki,&quot; and we know for sure Anu, Enlil, and Enki are three of these seven. Exactly who the other four are isn't really known, damaged tablets and that. In other periods (as soon as Babylon becomes the major city in the region) we see Marduk added to this list, while at other times they are a nebulous, featureless group of deities. Essentially, it seems to mean 'The Major Gods,' when it isn't being used as a semi-abstract concept.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Manannansun</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/155803-scion-2-0-project?p=1140701#post1140701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Since Brighid is the Irish Goddess prayed to for computer problems it would logical that a scion related to her would become the deity of the internet. So my question is how would you work such a individual considering 2.0 does not have a Forge purview?</div> </div> </div> </div>Internet and other technological advancements are all handled by Artistry. Think of Artistry as &quot;Forge and Art,&quot; folded up into a single Purview. Epic Intelligence has some Knacks that would help you with machines and controlling electronics, but due to the Tuatha's system of 'Professions as Gods,' you could manage it in a lot of ways. Artistry and Epic Intelligence will always be the major areas to work in. If the Scion is a hacker, consider Chaos, if they work with Augmented Reality, or Virtual Reality, consider adding Illusion, if they manage a CSIS-esque computer security situation, consider Justice, maybe Epic Perception if that idea includes mass observation.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-16T04:20:15-07:00","id":1140767,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/155803-scion-2-0-project?p=1140682#post1140682\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The actual 'truth' about the Sidhe is probably that it's more or less a word that means 'Non-Human Human-Like Supernatural Beings Who Reside In The Sidhe Mounds,' including the Deities, 'Fairy Queens' like Fand and Niamh, and minor entities like the Dullahan. It's less of a species, and more of a really broad term.<br /> <br /> However, that's literally an idea I just came up with right now inspired by how the Haida use a term roughly meaning 'Supernatural Beings' to describe everything from big Deities, to 'smaller' entities. Any interpretation of the Sidhe is okay more or less, they're a tricky thing to deal with. It's a term that has obviously changed meaning over the last thousand years.</div> </div> </div> </div>Certainly since the 1750s in Modern Irish the word has just meant &quot;non-angelic or non-demonic supernatural being&quot;, even ghosts were counted amoung the Sidhe and played in sports games on the Sidhe side such as in the folktale Taidhbhse an Chrainn (ghost of the tree).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3326-an-fhuiseog","name":"An Fhuiseog","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3326_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"271"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-17T13:01:09-08:00","id":1176824,"title":"","content":"Found one of my sources for Manannan mac Lir as a faerie king. Sorry it took me so long. I think you might find the following link of interest.<br /> <a href=\"https://manxwytch.wordpress.com/2012/06/19/midsummer-tributes/\" target=\"_blank\">https://manxwytch.wordpress.com/2012...mmer-tributes/</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13647-manannansun","name":"Manannansun","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"78"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}